; HetLitmus Layer-1 (Static) cram tests -- see hetlitmus/docs/TEST-PLAN.md sec 4.
; Pure bash/python, no toolchain, no GPU: they exercise the rule functions of
; _grid_lib.sh (basics.t) and ptxcheck.py's discriminating power on a frozen
; corrupt PTX (ptx-negatives.t).  Adapted from TEST-PLAN sec 6: the scripts under
; test live in parent dirs, so they are depended on by their real relative paths
; (the cram sandbox reconstructs the workspace tree and runs with cwd = this dir).
; The binaries (%{bin:...}) are intentionally omitted -- Layer 1 needs no OCaml
; build.
(cram
 (applies_to basics ptx-negatives)
 (deps
  ../_grid_lib.sh
  ../../verify/ptxcheck.py
  corrupt-strengthen.ptx
  (glob_files ../gpu-only/*.litmus)))

; pfix-cond.t, coop-launch.t and shared-alloc.t are the exceptions that DO need the
; OCaml build: they emit representative het tests through litmus7 and read the
; generated driver.  pfix-cond guards the condition compiler on location atoms;
; coop-launch guards the perpetual-loop + cooperative-launch driver shape;
; shared-alloc guards the per-target gd_alloc_shared / gd_free_shared allocator;
; stress guards the GPU memory-stress layer (device-memory scratchpad, pure-stress
; workgroups, and every scaffolding op confined to that scratchpad); cpu-stress
; guards the CPU + interconnect layer -- the host ISA kept out of the nvcc TU, the
; -2s invariants, the four object classes, and above all the stress_go ORDERING,
; which is the one property of that layer the liveness gate
; (hetlitmus/verify/cpustresscheck.py) structurally cannot see: its dynamic probe
; drives het_cpu_stress.h's mechanisms with its own flag and never exercises the
; emitted driver's orchestration.  Confirmed by bite test -- moving the stress_go
; raise after the joins passes cpustresscheck and fails here.
; Scoped so the three Layer-1 tests above stay toolchain-free.  litmus7 het emission
; needs no -set-libdir, so only the binary + the .litmus inputs are required.
; statistics.t guards the EMITTER half of the statistics layer.  The estimator's
; own behaviour is gated by hetlitmus/verify/statscheck.py (which compiles the real
; het_verdict.h and drives it with synthetic record streams); what cram pins is that
; the harness actually PRODUCES the inputs that layer consumes.
; slot-readout.t pins the per-iteration slot layout, the one-pass readout that
; reads iteration n's outcome out of slot n, and the condition compiler's two
; refusals -- an atom no outcome column backs, and a constant weak-behaviour
; detector -- each matched on the words only it prints.
(cram
 (applies_to pfix-cond coop-launch shared-alloc slot-readout stress cpu-stress
   statistics)
 (package herdtools7)
 (deps
  %{bin:litmus7}
  ; The whole corpus is staged: these tests emit from it, and naming each test
  ; here would silently break the moment one of them touched a different row.
  (glob_files ../het/*.litmus)
  ; Every .hip render in these tests comes from ../het-x86: a HIP harness is the
  ; (x86_64, hip) pair.
  (glob_files ../het-x86/*)
  ../gpu-only/MP-sys-acquire.litmus))

; gpu-target.t is separate because it is the only cram test that emits a PLAIN
; CPU-only test -- the boundary check that `-gpu-target' did not become a
; condition on upstream litmus7 usage.  That path needs litmus7's libdir, and the
; libdir cannot be a declared dep: its per-arch subdirectories are named
; `_aarch64', `_x86_64', ... and dune ignores directories starting with `_', so
; (source_tree ../../../litmus/libdir) stages the top level and NONE of the arch
; files -- silently, which is worse than an error.  So this test walks up from cwd
; to the source libdir instead, and the stanza above stays as it was.
(cram
 (applies_to gpu-target)
 (package herdtools7)
 (deps
  %{bin:litmus7}
  (glob_files ../het/*.litmus)
  ; The HIP half of this test emits from the (x86_64, hip) pair.  ../het-x86 is
  ; the committed fixture for that pair -- see its README for why the generated
  ; x86 corpus is not usable here (generate-x86.sh needs hetgen7 on $PATH).
  (glob_files ../het-x86/*)
  ../gpu-only/MP-sys-acquire.litmus))
